  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Wednesday)...\n\nA persistent weather pattern with an upper low over the west \ncoast and an omega block ridge over the midwest will continue to \ndominate the weather picture the next few days, slowly sliding \neastward by the weekend. While this pattern has pumped higher \nPWATs into our region, the upper low remains to far west to eject \nany decent energy or perturbations across our region. This keeps \nthe stronger storms and better coverage to our west. This will \ncontinue to be the case for today where only isolated late day \nthunderstorms are expected. A few gusts to 45 mph or small hail \nare still possible...as is a quick brief downpour with a few \ncells. High temperatures will range from mid 80s to lower 90s \nagain. East to southeast winds will be brisk over the eastern \nplains (Miles City, Baker) where gusts between 20-40 mph are \nexpected.\n\nModels suggest the upper low will slowly shift east through \nNevada and Utah on Friday. This will bring the better forcing\ncloser to our region by late Friday. A few stronger storms and \nsomewhat better coverage is suggested in our western CWA. We could\nsee an isolated threat of gusts to 60+ mph and possibly hail,\nespecially NW of Billings in the Wheatland County vicinity. Temps\nmay reach the warmest of the week Friday, with highs in the upper\n80s to mid 90s. The associated thermal trough will shift east as \nwell which should result in diminished winds out east over the \nplains. \n\nModels continue to indicate the upper low will lift from the\ncentral Rockies into Montana Friday night into Saturday. Some \nsolutions show a weakening low lifting into Canada, while others \nwrap it up over our region for a few days. Latest cluster \nanalysis favor the latter solution (60% chance). This would \npotentially lead to an extended rainy and cooler period for \nsouthern MT and northern WY. We could see a threat of severe \nthunderstorms out east Saturday, with otherwise showers and \nembedded thunder prevailing. Another wave could move in behind the\nsystem into early next week, though it remains more uncertain. At\nany rate, there is increasing confidence in cooler conditions and\nincreasing shower and thunderstorm activity this weekend. \nCurrently, the probability for 0.5+ inches of precipitation \nSaturday thru Monday ranges from 40-80%, greatest over western \nand central areas. Temperatures are forecast to drop into the \nupper 60s to 80s Saturday, to the 60s to 70s Sunday through \nTuesday. BT",
